# Summary
Introduced: routing object version allocation through a separate metadata command adds a new failure mode where a partially applied ReserveObjectVersion command is unrecoverable after coordinator restart because only command indexes, not pending commands, are reconstructed. The strict per-replica equality check then turns the inconsistent counters into persistent write failures for versioned operations on the affected object key.
Version allocation is now split into a separate metadata command. The coordinator records the command only in the process-local pending_metadata_commands map, applies it to replicas, and removes it on success. If a process crash or restart happens after the ReserveObjectVersion command has been applied to some, but not all, acting-set nodes, that pending command is not recovered. On restart the command index is seeded from the maximum durable log index, but the lost command is not replayed or drained. A subsequent reservation computes the next version_id from the current primary only. Replicas that did apply the lost reservation will have a higher next_version_id, while replicas that did not will have a lower one. The new reserve_object_version_explicit implementation requires exact equality with each replica's local next_version_id and rejects mismatches as stale, so the new reservation fails. Repeated retries can continue to pick a version_id that at least one replica rejects, leaving versioning-enabled writes and delete-marker insertions for that key unavailable until manual metadata repair.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm version allocation is split into a standalone `ReserveObjectVersion` command stored only in process-local pending state before apply.
- [x] Confirm metadata command application can durably affect only a subset of acting-set replicas before returning an error.
- [x] Confirm restart/open reconstructs command indexes but not pending metadata commands.
- [x] Reproduce divergent object version counters after partial apply plus reopen.
- [x] Demonstrate subsequent reservations repeatedly fail with `reserve object version command stale version` rather than repairing divergence.
## Report
Validated the finding with a focused Rust unit-test PoC plus LLDB trace. I added a targeted test in `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs` that: (1) creates a 3-node local cluster with primary node 1, (2) injects a `ReserveObjectVersion` apply failure on the primary after non-primary replicas have already applied, (3) confirms the same-process in-memory pending command exists and counters diverge, (4) drops and reopens the cluster to simulate restart, (5) confirms `pending_metadata_commands` is gone while durable counters remain split, and (6) retries reservation twice and gets persistent stale-version errors.

Relevant code evidence: `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1022-1059` stores a new `ReserveObjectVersion` only in process-local pending state and then applies it; `crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:679-711` applies metadata commands sequentially to acting-set nodes, allowing partial durable apply before a later failure; `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:248-263` initializes `pending_metadata_commands` to an empty `HashMap` on runtime construction; `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:1236-1262` seeds only max command indexes on open; `crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:5881-5884` rejects explicit reservation when local `expected != version_id` with `reserve object version command stale version`.

Focused command: `cargo test -p storage reserve_object_version_partial_apply_lost_after_reopen_causes_stale_retry -- --nocapture`. Output showed: `initial partial apply error: Store(Io { context: "injected lost reserve object version apply failure", ... })`, then after reopen: `post-reopen attempt 1 error: Metadata(Db { context: "reserve object version command stale version", source: InvalidQuery })` and `post-reopen attempt 2 error: Metadata(Db { context: "reserve object version command stale version", source: InvalidQuery })`, with the test passing. This confirms the post-restart state is unrepaired and repeated retries fail.

Debugger validation: `gdb` was not installed, so I used LLDB. Breakpoint at `crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:5883` hit exactly at the stale-version return. Backtrace showed the chain `PgStore::reserve_object_version_explicit` -> `apply_reserve_object_version_command` -> `apply_metadata_command_and_record` -> `StorageCluster::apply_metadata_command_to_acting_set_from_origin` -> `apply_new_object_metadata_command_for_bucket` -> `StorageCluster::reserve_next_object_version` -> the PoC test. This demonstrates the new reservation after restart reaches a replica with a mismatched local counter and is rejected.

Crash/Valgrind attempts: a direct process crash is not expected because this is a persistent logic/availability bug, not memory corruption; the focused test reproduced the logical failure instead of crashing. `valgrind --version` returned exit 127 (`command not found`).

