# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Kept at medium. Repository evidence confirms this is not a false positive: production S3 PutObject/DeleteObject/multipart paths call reserve_next_object_version for enabled-versioning buckets; that function stores ReserveObjectVersion only in an in-memory pending map; metadata command apply is sequential and can be partially durable; restart reconstructs only indexes and an empty pending map; and pg_store rejects later reservations when replica counters disagree. The issue is in-scope because it affects the argmin-s3 runtime storage path and the threat model includes versioning metadata and availability. It is not high/critical because it requires write/delete capability plus a crash/I/O-failure timing window, and the impact is persistent availability loss for affected key operations rather than auth bypass, data exfiltration, RCE, or broad cross-tenant compromise.
## Likelihood
low - The normal API path is reachable to permitted S3 writers, but exploitation requires a versioning-enabled bucket and a narrow operational-fault window where ReserveObjectVersion is durably applied to only part of the acting set before process restart. The remote client controls the write/delete operation but not necessarily the crash or I/O failure, so exploitation is high complexity.
## Impact
medium - A successful trigger can persistently prevent versioned writes and delete-marker insertion for the affected object key until metadata repair. It does not expose secrets, bypass authentication, execute code, or directly corrupt arbitrary objects, and the demonstrated impact is availability rather than confidentiality or broad integrity compromise.
## Assumptions
- The production service is the argmin-s3 binary described by README and guides/threat_model.md.
- An attacker is a client who can reach the S3-compatible HTTP endpoint and has write/delete capability for a versioning-enabled bucket, either through SigV4 credentials, bucket policy, or public-write ACL configuration.
- The attacker cannot directly call internal storage APIs; the partial-apply window requires an operational fault such as process crash/restart, node/storage I/O error, or similar failure during metadata command application.
- No deployment manifests or load balancer configuration were present in the repository checkout, so public exposure is inferred only from application defaults and configurable listen address.
- argmin-s3 runtime is serving the S3 HTTP API
- target bucket has versioning enabled
- attacker can issue PutObject, CopyObject, multipart completion, or DeleteObject that allocates an object version for the target key
- ReserveObjectVersion applies to only some acting-set nodes
- process crashes or restarts before the in-memory pending metadata command is recovered or drained
- later version allocation for the same key encounters divergent replica next_version_id counters
## Path
Client -> HTTP S3 API -> Coordinator/storage versioned write
       -> ReserveObjectVersion pending in RAM
       -> partial replica apply + crash/restart
       -> pending map empty, counters split
       -> stale-version rejection -> key-level write/delete-marker DoS
