## Narrative
The finding is real and in scope: production HTTP S3 operations can reach version allocation for enabled-versioning buckets, and the storage layer records a ReserveObjectVersion command only in process-local pending state before applying it to replicas. The apply loop can persist the command to some nodes before failing. On reopen, local runtime state initializes pending_metadata_commands to an empty HashMap and startup seeds only max command indexes, so the partially applied command is not replayed. Later reservations use the primary's next_version_id and PgStore rejects replicas whose local expected version differs, producing persistent stale-version failures. Severity remains medium, not high or critical, because impact is availability-only and appears limited to the affected key/bucket operations, while exploitation requires write/delete authorization plus a high-complexity crash/I/O-failure timing window that the remote client does not fully control.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication for signed requests
- Anonymous access only for ACL/policy-permitted public paths
- server-core bucket/object authorization
- OwnerAccountAdmin credential loaded from environment
- Default listener is 127.0.0.1:9000
- Optional direct TLS via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
- max_connections and max_inflight_requests admission control
- Per-PG and metadata command apply locking
## Blindspots
- No Kubernetes, Terraform, Docker, or load balancer manifests were present, so actual internet exposure cannot be established from repository artifacts.
- This static pass did not rerun the validation PoC; it relies on the provided validation evidence plus repository code review.
- The repository shows normal API reachability, but does not prove a remote client alone can force the required partial-apply crash/I/O failure without an external operational fault.
- The exact blast radius depends on bucket policy/ACL configuration and whether untrusted users have write/delete permissions to versioning-enabled buckets.
- Local-node behavior was reviewed; future multihost deployment details may change failure probability or exposure.
